Make TurboQuant's boundary protection reachable, and fix the keyword crash

Two changes to `--kv-cache-dtype-skip-layers`, one a bug fix the other a lever.

**The crash.** The flag documents that it "accepts layer indices (e.g. '0', '2',
'4') or attention type names (e.g. 'sliding_window')", and `Attention.__init__`
does implement the `sliding_window` keyword. But with a `turboquant_*` cache
dtype the flag's own value is merged with TurboQuant's automatic boundary skips
by `sorted(existing | set(boundary), key=int)`, and `int('sliding_window')`
raises. So the documented invocation has never worked on the one dtype whose
users most need it: a sliding-window model must hold its sliding layers native,
because TurboQuant cannot serve a sliding window. Fixed with a sort key that
orders indices numerically ahead of keywords instead of assuming everything is a
number.

**The lever.** `TurboQuantConfig.get_boundary_skip_layers(model_config, n=2)`
leaves the first and last N layers on a native cache. The parameter is already
there; the sole call site hardcodes it, and the flag only ever *adds* to the
resulting list -- so no invocation can reduce or retarget boundary protection.
This adds `boundary:N` to the flag's existing keyword vocabulary, resolved into
layer indices while the engine config is built, so nothing downstream sees a new
kind of entry. `boundary:0` disables protection; `boundary:1` keeps one layer at
each end. Passing it with a non-TurboQuant dtype is an error rather than silently
ignored.

Read declaratively -- the list specifies which layers keep a native cache, with
an implicit `boundary:2` -- this reads as one more specifier rather than a
subtraction, and it composes:

    --kv-cache-dtype-skip-layers sliding_window boundary:0

is the whole configuration for a sliding-window model with no boundary
protection, which today cannot be expressed at all and until this patch could
not even be typed.

**Why it is worth reaching.** Measured on Qwen/Qwen3-4B, GSM8K 5-shot, greedy,
full test set n=1319, paired per-item (see docs/turboquant-kv.md):

  - The protection is real and the existing docstring is right: removing it costs
    k3v4_nc 29.80 points, matching its "~30 points on Qwen3-4B". 4-bit is not
    exempt either, -6.52 (p=5e-08).
  - But layer 0 is essentially the whole effect. Protecting only the trailing
    layers {34,35} is indistinguishable from protecting nothing (p=0.79), while
    costing two layers of native cache. Protecting {0} alone is indistinguishable
    from the full stock set (p=0.17) at 18% fewer bytes.
  - Consequently both aggressive presets are dominated as they ship: 3bit_nc at
    78.17% and 42,496 B/token is beaten by 4bit_nc protecting only layer 0, which
    scores 83.17% at 41,616 -- five points better at fewer bytes.

The default is unchanged at N=2. This only makes the rest of the curve
expressible.

